 <div aria-labelledby="model-tab-hardware" class="model-track-panel" data-model-panel="hardware" id="model-hardware-simulations">
 <section class="section-block model-track-overview" id="hardware-model-summary"><span class="section-eyebrow">Hardware simulations / Summary</span><h2 class="reading-heading--chapter">Physical model overview</h2><p>The gas model predicts a four-well surface t90 of 47.80–49.42 s at 3 mL/min, D = 10 mm²/s and a 100 µL fill. Conditional optical transport predicts D3 detector collection of 0.593 ± 0.035 nW. We use these separate calculations to locate transport and readout limits before interpreting a biological signal.</p></section>
 <section class="section-block model-v2-physical model-v2-intro" data-reveal="" id="physical-models"><span class="section-eyebrow">Hardware simulations / Physical models</span><h2 class="reading-heading--chapter">Transport and measurement assumptions</h2><p data-study-context="physical-models">Molecules must reach the cells and emitted light must reach the detector. We model these steps separately to locate limits from optical collection, excitation leakage, gas distribution and liquid transport.</p><p>These physical models quantify gas delivery to the liquid interface, fluorescence collection and optical interference in the reader design.</p><p>This study concerns the proposed reader’s physical signal path. <a href="#model-odor-decoding">Odor decoding is a separate study of metal-oxide sensor data.</a></p><div class="model-v2-questions"><a href="#gas-and-liquid-transport"><span>01–02 / TRANSPORT</span><strong>How does the sample reach the cells?</strong><small>Gas arrival, liquid depth and membrane delay →</small></a><a href="#optical-transport"><span>03 / LIGHT</span><strong>Which photons reach the detector?</strong><small>Geometry, sampled paths and optical mixing →</small></a><a href="#detector-interference"><span>04 / FILTER</span><strong>Why choose one LEE 101 layer?</strong><small>All four filter cases, spectra and interference →</small></a></div></section>
-<section class="section-block model-v2-physical" data-reveal="" id="gas-and-liquid-transport"><span class="section-eyebrow">Hardware simulations / 01</span><h2 class="reading-heading--chapter">Gas arrival at the wells</h2><p data-study-context="gas-and-liquid-transport">We compared chamber geometry and flow under the same inlet concentration step to test arrival time and delivery uniformity. Maps locate delayed regions; surface curves show timing and spatial spread across wells.</p><dl class="audit-nutshell"><div><dt>Inputs</dt><dd>Integral shelf, nominal CAD and 100 µL per well</dd></div><div><dt>Method</dt><dd>Gas flow and advection–diffusion</dd></div><div><dt>Output</dt><dd>Gas-side surface concentration and arrival time</dd></div></dl><p>Arrival is measured from a concentration step at the modeled inlet to the area-mean gas concentration in the first layer above the liquid. The calculation stops at the gas side of that interface; it does not predict uptake into the medium or the cell response.</p><p>We first compared how a concentration step moves through the chamber. The movie holds the color scale fixed across geometries and flows; the maps below resolve that progression in a vertical section and immediately above each well.</p><figure class="model-v2-media" data-model-video="" data-static-video-number="1" id="surface-arrival-simulation-video"><div class="model-v2-media__label"><span>Gas concentration at the liquid surfaces</span><span>Simulation · 60 s</span></div><iframe allow="autoplay; fullscreen; picture-in-picture" allowfullscreen="" aria-describedby="surface-arrival-video-caption" data-igem-video="" loading="lazy" src="https://video.igem.org/videos/embed/7cb56939-73ca-4209-85d2-9731fd62efda?p2p=0" style="display:block;width:100%;height:auto;aspect-ratio:10 / 7;border:0" title="Gas concentration at the liquid surfaces"></iframe><figcaption id="surface-arrival-video-caption"><strong class="media-caption__label">Video 1. </strong>Geometry comparison at 3 mL/min, followed by the selected shelf at 1, 3 and 5 mL/min. Each chapter shows 180 physical seconds in 30 seconds of playback and uses the same concentration scale. The clock resets between chapters. Maps and curves use computed gas concentrations; the liquid is a stationary boundary.</figcaption><noscript><p><a href="#model-data-availability">Data and methods</a></p></noscript></figure><figure class="hw-v2-fig" data-static-figure-number="1" id="model-surface-concentration"><a href="https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6460/wiki/hardware/expanded-verification/surface-concentration-d-channels-0764eaf4ff4d.webp" rel="noopener" target="_blank"><img alt="Gas concentration in the northern section and at all four liquid surfaces after 10, 30 and 60 seconds" decoding="async" height="1386" loading="lazy" src="https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6460/wiki/hardware/expanded-verification/surface-concentration-d-channels-0764eaf4ff4d.webp" width="2332"/></a><figcaption><strong class="media-caption__label">Figure 1. </strong>At 3 mL/min, D = 10 mm²/s and 100 µL per well, a vertical section and the first gas-cell layers show concentration after 10, 30 and 60 s. Every panel uses C/Cin from 0 to 1. Blank regions are outside the gas domain; values are gas concentrations, not concentrations in the medium.</figcaption></figure><p>The maps reveal where gas remains dilute, but choosing a flow also requires a time threshold. We therefore tracked each well’s surface-area mean as it approaches the inlet concentration; the shaded curves retain spatial variation around that mean.</p><figure class="hw-v2-fig hw-design-result" data-static-figure-number="2" id="model-surface-arrival-curves"><a href="https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6460/wiki/hardware/expanded-verification/surface-arrival-d-channels-54a6e176a9a3.webp" rel="noopener" target="_blank"><img alt="Liquid-adjacent gas concentration over time and slowest-well arrival time across flow rates and gas diffusivities." decoding="async" height="924" loading="lazy" src="https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6460/wiki/hardware/expanded-verification/surface-arrival-d-channels-54a6e176a9a3.webp" width="2288"/></a><figcaption><strong class="media-caption__label">Figure 2. </strong>Gas arrival at the liquid surface. Curves show surface-area mean concentration; shading spans the spatial 10th–90th percentiles. Nominal conditions are 3 mL/min, gas diffusivity 10 mm²/s and a 0.4 mm grid. Time starts at the modeled inlet concentration step. These are gas-arrival times, not cellular response times.</figcaption></figure><p>At <strong>3 mL/min</strong> and nominal gas diffusivity <strong>10 mm²/s</strong>, the four surfaces reach 10% of inlet concentration in <strong>5.30–6.29 s</strong> and 90% in <strong>47.80–49.42 s</strong>. Across the three tested diffusivities, the slowest surface reaches 90% in approximately <strong>50 s</strong> on the main grid.</p><p>Geometry was compared at the same flow and diffusivity to separate redistribution from simply pumping faster. Read each interval from t10 to t90 and compare its t50 marker: the shelf redirects gas without giving the shortest interval in every case.</p><figure class="hw-v2-fig" data-static-figure-number="3" id="model-surface-geometry"><a href="https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6460/wiki/hardware/expanded-verification/surface-comparison-d-channels-30fbad3d8a86.webp" rel="noopener" target="_blank"><img alt="Gas-surface concentration and rise-time intervals for the open chamber, perforated plate and integral shelf" decoding="async" height="924" loading="lazy" src="https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6460/wiki/hardware/expanded-verification/surface-comparison-d-channels-30fbad3d8a86.webp" width="2288"/></a><figcaption><strong class="media-caption__label">Figure 3. </strong>At Q = 3 mL/min and D = 10 mm²/s, the slowest surface t90 is 51.67 s for the open chamber, 48.36 s for the original 12-hole plate and 49.42 s for the shelf. The intervals show t10–t90, with t50 marked by a dot; they are not error bars. Small differences on one grid do not establish a physical ranking.</figcaption></figure><p>For the selected shelf, the slowest surface t90 is <strong>149.3, 49.4 and 29.9 s</strong> at 1, 3 and 5 mL/min. The 3 mL/min candidate retains timing margin without treating faster arrival as the only design criterion. The geometry comparison and numerical sensitivity checks below test the effects of chamber design and discretization.</p><p>Finally, flow and diffusivity sweeps test the operating range, while grid and time-step checks show how much the timing depends on discretization. The small separation between geometries must be read alongside these numerical shifts.</p><figure class="hw-v2-fig" data-static-figure-number="4" id="model-surface-sensitivity"><a href="https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6460/wiki/aerosense-assets-20261017/lossless-part2-20261018/b16507b9e462-surface-sensitivity.webp" rel="noopener" target="_blank"><img alt="Twenty-one first-layer gas transport cases and grid and time-step sensitivity checks" decoding="async" height="924" loading="lazy" src="https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6460/wiki/aerosense-assets-20261017/lossless-part2-20261018/b16507b9e462-surface-sensitivity.webp" width="2530"/></a><figcaption><strong class="media-caption__label">Figure 4. </strong>First-layer timing across seven flows and three diffusivities. At Q = 3 mL/min and D = 20 mm²/s, t90 is 50.024 s on the main grid, 51.010 s at 0.3 mm, 50.499 s at 0.2 mm and 49.872 s with the main-grid time step halved. Grid changes also change the stair-step geometry; these are numerical sensitivity checks, not experimental uncertainty intervals.</figcaption></figure><p class="supplementary-data__link"><a href="#model-surface-methods">Surface results and numerical checks</a> · <a href="#gas-and-liquid-transport-details">Geometry sweeps and operating-band results</a> · <a href="#liquid-transport">Liquid and membrane screening</a></p><p><a class="hw-v2-text-link" href="hardware.html#gas-path-results">Hardware: selected gas path and chamber geometry →</a></p></section>
+<section class="section-block model-v2-physical" data-reveal="" id="gas-and-liquid-transport"><span class="section-eyebrow">Hardware simulations / 01</span><h2 class="reading-heading--chapter">Gas arrival at the wells</h2><p data-study-context="gas-and-liquid-transport">We compared chamber geometry and flow under the same inlet concentration step to test arrival time and delivery uniformity. Maps locate delayed regions; surface curves show timing and spatial spread across wells.</p><dl class="audit-nutshell"><div><dt>Inputs</dt><dd>Integral shelf, nominal CAD and 100 µL per well</dd></div><div><dt>Method</dt><dd>Gas flow and advection–diffusion</dd></div><div><dt>Output</dt><dd>Gas-side surface concentration and arrival time</dd></div></dl><p>Arrival is measured from a concentration step at the modeled inlet to the area-mean gas concentration in the first layer above the liquid. The calculation stops at the gas side of that interface; it does not predict uptake into the medium or the cell response.</p><p>We first compared how a concentration step moves through the chamber. The movie holds the color scale fixed across geometries and flows; the maps below resolve that progression in a vertical section and immediately above each well.</p><figure class="model-v2-media" data-model-video="" data-static-video-number="1" id="surface-arrival-simulation-video"><div class="model-v2-media__label"><span>Gas concentration at the liquid surfaces</span><span>Simulation · 60 s</span></div><iframe allow="autoplay; fullscreen; picture-in-picture" allowfullscreen="" aria-describedby="surface-arrival-video-caption" data-igem-video="" loading="lazy" src="https://video.igem.org/videos/embed/7cb56939-73ca-4209-85d2-9731fd62efda?p2p=0" style="display:block;width:100%;height:auto;aspect-ratio:10 / 7;border:0" title="Gas concentration at the liquid surfaces"></iframe><figcaption id="surface-arrival-video-caption"><strong class="media-caption__label">Video 1. </strong>Geometry comparison at 3 mL/min, followed by the selected shelf at 1, 3 and 5 mL/min. Each chapter shows 180 physical seconds in 30 seconds of playback and uses the same concentration scale. The clock resets between chapters. Maps and curves use computed gas concentrations; the liquid is a stationary boundary.</figcaption><noscript><p><a href="#model-data-availability">Data and methods</a></p></noscript></figure><figure class="hw-v2-fig" data-static-figure-number="1" id="model-surface-concentration"><a href="https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6460/wiki/hardware/expanded-verification/surface-concentration-d-channels-0764eaf4ff4d.webp" rel="noopener" target="_blank"><img alt="Gas concentration in the northern section and at all four liquid surfaces after 10, 30 and 60 seconds" decoding="async" height="1386" loading="lazy" src="https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6460/wiki/hardware/expanded-verification/surface-concentration-d-channels-0764eaf4ff4d.webp" width="2332"/></a><figcaption><strong class="media-caption__label">Figure 1. </strong>At 3 mL/min, D = 10 mm²/s and 100 µL per well, a vertical section and the first gas-cell layers show concentration after 10, 30 and 60 s. Every panel uses C/Cin from 0 to 1. Blank regions are outside the gas domain; values are gas concentrations, not concentrations in the medium.</figcaption></figure><p>The maps reveal where gas remains dilute, but choosing a flow also requires a time threshold. We therefore tracked each well’s surface-area mean as it approaches the inlet concentration; the shaded curves retain spatial variation around that mean.</p><figure class="hw-v2-fig hw-design-result" data-static-figure-number="2" id="model-surface-arrival-curves"><a href="https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6460/wiki/hardware/expanded-verification/surface-arrival-d-channels-54a6e176a9a3.webp" rel="noopener" target="_blank"><img alt="Liquid-adjacent gas concentration over time and slowest-well arrival time across flow rates and gas diffusivities." decoding="async" height="924" loading="lazy" src="https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6460/wiki/hardware/expanded-verification/surface-arrival-d-channels-54a6e176a9a3.webp" width="2288"/></a><figcaption><strong class="media-caption__label">Figure 2. </strong>Gas arrival at the liquid surface. Curves show surface-area mean concentration; shading spans the spatial 10th–90th percentiles. Nominal conditions are 3 mL/min, gas diffusivity 10 mm²/s and a 0.4 mm grid. Time starts at the modeled inlet concentration step. These are gas-arrival times, not cellular response times.</figcaption></figure><p>At <strong>3 mL/min</strong> and nominal gas diffusivity <strong>10 mm²/s</strong>, the four surfaces reach 10% of inlet concentration in <strong>5.30–6.29 s</strong> and 90% in <strong>47.80–49.42 s</strong>. Across the three tested diffusivities, the slowest surface reaches 90% in approximately <strong>50 s</strong> on the main grid.</p><p>Geometry was compared at the same flow and diffusivity to separate redistribution from simply pumping faster. Read each interval from t10 to t90 and compare its t50 marker: the shelf redirects gas without giving the shortest interval in every case.</p><figure class="hw-v2-fig" data-static-figure-number="3" id="model-surface-geometry"><a href="https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6460/wiki/hardware/expanded-verification/surface-comparison-d-channels-30fbad3d8a86.webp" rel="noopener" target="_blank"><img alt="Gas-surface concentration and rise-time intervals for the open chamber, perforated plate and integral shelf" decoding="async" height="924" loading="lazy" src="https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6460/wiki/hardware/expanded-verification/surface-comparison-d-channels-30fbad3d8a86.webp" width="2288"/></a><figcaption><strong class="media-caption__label">Figure 3. </strong>At Q = 3 mL/min and D = 10 mm²/s, the slowest surface t90 is 51.67 s for the open chamber, 48.36 s for the original 12-hole plate and 49.42 s for the shelf. The intervals show t10–t90, with t50 marked by a dot; they are not error bars. Small differences on one grid do not establish a physical ranking.</figcaption></figure><p>For the selected shelf, the slowest surface t90 is <strong>149.3, 49.4 and 29.9 s</strong> at 1, 3 and 5 mL/min. The 3 mL/min candidate retains timing margin without treating faster arrival as the only design criterion. The geometry comparison and numerical sensitivity checks below test the effects of chamber design and discretization.</p><p>Finally, flow and diffusivity sweeps test the operating range, while grid and time-step checks show how much the timing depends on discretization. The small separation between geometries must be read alongside these numerical shifts.</p><figure class="hw-v2-fig" data-static-figure-number="4" id="model-surface-sensitivity"><a href="https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6460/wiki/aerosense-assets-20261017/lossless-part2-20261018/b16507b9e462-surface-sensitivity.webp" rel="noopener" target="_blank"><img alt="Twenty-one first-layer gas transport cases and grid and time-step sensitivity checks" decoding="async" height="924" loading="lazy" src="https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6460/wiki/aerosense-assets-20261017/lossless-part2-20261018/b16507b9e462-surface-sensitivity.webp" width="2530"/></a><figcaption><strong class="media-caption__label">Figure 4. </strong>First-layer timing across seven flows and three diffusivities. At Q = 3 mL/min and D = 20 mm²/s, t90 is 50.024 s on the main grid, 51.010 s at 0.3 mm, 50.499 s at 0.2 mm and 49.872 s with the main-grid time step halved. Grid changes also change the stair-step geometry; these are numerical sensitivity checks, not experimental uncertainty intervals.</figcaption></figure></section>
 <section class="section-block model-v2-physical" id="liquid-transport"><span class="section-eyebrow">Hardware simulations / 02</span><h2 class="reading-heading--chapter">Liquid and membrane transport</h2><p data-study-context="liquid-transport">Gas must also cross the liquid to reach bottom-attached cells. We varied liquid depth and diffusivity before adding a membrane; compare depth effects first, then the membrane’s added delay.</p><p>The first comparison places gas-side wash-in and delivery to the well floor on their respective time axes. It tests whether chamber equilibration is a useful proxy for exposure of bottom-attached cells.</p><figure class="hw-v2-fig" data-static-figure-number="5" id="liquid-delivery-result"><a href="https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6460/wiki/aerosense-assets-20261017/lossless-part2-20261018/e30c793d548e-01-gas-and-liquid-times.webp" rel="noopener" target="_blank"><img alt="Separate time scales for gas-interface wash-in and liquid-bottom delivery" decoding="async" loading="lazy" src="https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6460/wiki/aerosense-assets-20261017/lossless-part2-20261018/e30c793d548e-01-gas-and-liquid-times.webp"/></a><figcaption><strong class="media-caption__label">Figure 5. </strong> Gas and liquid transport have different time scales. At 8.929 mm depth, bottom t90 is 45.67, 22.83 and 11.42 h for D = 0.5, 1 and 2 × 10⁻⁹ m²/s. These deterministic, unmixed surface-step calculations describe transport to the bottom, not receptor or GCaMP response time.</figcaption></figure><p>At 100 µL per well, the nominal liquid depth is 8.929 mm. With an ideal concentration step at the surface, no mixing and liquid diffusivity D = 10⁻⁹ m²/s, the model gives a bottom t90 of 22.83 h. The relation t90 ≈ 1.031105 L²/D makes liquid depth a strong design variable: halving the path reduces this idealized time fourfold. <a aria-label="Reference 1" class="cite" data-model-reference="ref-firpo-2019" href="#ref-firpo-2019" id="cite-firpo-model-membrane">[1]</a></p><p>Depth and effective diffusivity were varied independently to ask which design change could shorten that path. The depth sweep tests geometric distance; the mixing multiplier represents a hypothetical transport enhancement.</p><figure class="hw-v2-fig" data-static-figure-number="6" id="model-liquid-depth"><a href="https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6460/wiki/aerosense-assets-20261017/lossless-part2-20261018/9d744b215656-02-depth-and-mixing.webp" rel="noopener" target="_blank"><img alt="Sensitivity of bottom delivery to liquid depth and an assumed effective mixing factor" decoding="async" loading="lazy" src="https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6460/wiki/aerosense-assets-20261017/lossless-part2-20261018/9d744b215656-02-depth-and-mixing.webp"/></a><figcaption><strong class="media-caption__label">Figure 6. </strong> Bottom delivery scales with liquid depth squared and inversely with diffusivity. A 25 µL counterfactual gives about 1.43 h at D = 10⁻⁹ m²/s; the selected fill remains 100 µL. Increased effective diffusivity represents an assumed mixing effect, not an implemented mixing mechanism.</figcaption></figure><p>A membrane adds another barrier whose importance may depend on liquid mixing. The thickness–diffusivity–partitioning sweep separates those regimes: color tracks delay, while gray marks conditions that do not reach the target during the simulated window.</p><figure class="hw-v2-fig" data-static-figure-number="7" id="membrane-sweep-result"><a href="https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6460/wiki/aerosense-assets-20261017/lossless-part2-20261018/d9720356d6f8-04-pdms-coupled-sweep.webp" rel="noopener" target="_blank"><img alt="Coupled membrane and liquid sensitivity across the assumed parameter grid" decoding="async" loading="lazy" src="https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6460/wiki/aerosense-assets-20261017/lossless-part2-20261018/d9720356d6f8-04-pdms-coupled-sweep.webp"/></a><figcaption><strong class="media-caption__label">Figure 7. </strong> PDMS thickness, diffusivity and partitioning jointly control delay. The 40 displayed cells are a slice of 243 deterministic conditions, at 100 µL fill, liquid D = 10⁻⁹ m²/s and liquid–gas partition coefficient 10. Gray cells did not reach 90% within 168 h. Material inputs are illustrative; the model does not measure target-specific permeability or cell stability.</figcaption></figure><p>The coupled PDMS sweep tests 243 material and geometry combinations. A representative 100 µm film changes t90 from 22.85 to 23.39 h under stagnant-liquid assumptions; with liquid diffusion increased 100-fold, the same comparison becomes 0.25 versus 0.85 h. The liquid can dominate one regime and the membrane another. This supports the no-film baseline until a target molecule and measured permeability justify adding a layer.</p><p><a class="hw-v2-text-link" href="hardware.html#membrane-design-results">Hardware: selected no-film chamber and sample geometry →</a></p></section>
-<section class="section-block model-v2-physical" data-reveal="" id="optical-transport"><span class="section-eyebrow">Hardware simulations / 03</span><h2 class="reading-heading--chapter">Trace excitation and fluorescence</h2><p data-study-context="optical-transport">We varied optical geometry to test fluorescence collection and source-well mixing at the shared detector. Compare collection with spatial variation, then follow each LED row across the mixing matrix to separate intended-well signal from crosstalk.</p><dl class="audit-nutshell"><div><dt>Inputs</dt><dd>CAD, 500 µW LED-on reference, assumed optical properties</dd></div><div><dt>Method</dt><dd>Finite-detector Monte Carlo photon transport</dd></div><div><dt>Output</dt><dd>Collection, spatial variation and well mixing</dd></div><div><dt>Decision</dt><dd>Fix registration and calibrate each well</dd></div></dl><p>Geometry reference. The 470/520 nm study and path movie use monochromatic transport. The separate spectral comparison weights unfiltered Monte Carlo inputs by wavelength; the two studies report different power and noise quantities. <a href="#detector-interference">spectral filter comparison</a></p><p>The Monte Carlo geometry contains four registered wells, a finite offset photodiode, a finite emission filter and specified resin/liquid interfaces. Excitation and fluorescence packets are scored separately at nominal wavelengths of 470 and 520 nm. The reference source is a 20 µm layer at the well floor with absorption 0.5 mm<sup>−1</sup>, quantum yield 1 and 500 µW LED-on power. Ideal clear resin is an explicit assumption, not a measured material specification.</p><p>The optical maps follow Burton et al.’s separation of excitation fluence and detector acceptance (Fig. 2H–J). AeroSense computes its own four-well geometry; none of the paper’s device performance is transferred. <a aria-label="Reference 2" class="cite" data-model-reference="ref-burton-2020" href="#ref-burton-2020" id="cite-burton-model-method">[2]</a></p><figure class="hw-v2-fig" data-static-figure-number="8" id="model-optical-parameter-sweep"><a href="https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6460/wiki/hardware/expanded-verification/01-parameter-sweep-d-channels-10d0b10cbe4c.webp" rel="noopener" target="_blank"><img alt="Current optical parameter sweep with three-seed uncertainty" decoding="async" loading="lazy" src="https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6460/wiki/hardware/expanded-verification/01-parameter-sweep-d-channels-10d0b10cbe4c.webp"/></a><figcaption><strong class="media-caption__label">Figure 8. </strong>Current optical sensitivity study. Every comparison uses the stated geometry and source assumptions; error bars represent finite Monte Carlo sampling.</figcaption></figure><figure class="hw-v2-fig" data-static-figure-number="9" id="well-mixing-result"><a href="https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6460/wiki/review-resources/optical-calibration/canonical-simulation.svg" rel="noopener" target="_blank"><img alt="Canonical optical simulation matrix: row-normalized response percentages for readout D1 to D4 and source wells D1 to D4" decoding="async" loading="lazy" src="https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6460/wiki/review-resources/optical-calibration/canonical-simulation.svg"/></a><figcaption><strong class="media-caption__label">Figure 9. </strong><strong>Simulation · canonical optical mixing.</strong> This is the same floor-layer design model used in Hardware. Each row is normalized to 100%; for example, D4 own-well power 0.657 nW becomes 90.9% of its row total. Rows are LED / readout channels and columns are fluorescent source wells. The original three-seed matrix and its 20 µm source layer, absorption coefficient 0.5 mm⁻¹ and geometry are unchanged. <a href="hardware.html#reader-simulation-comparison">Measured comparison</a></figcaption></figure><p>The measured comparison pairs all 16 corresponding entries of the simulated and measured row-normalized matrices. The scatter plot uses simulation on the horizontal axis and measurement on the vertical axis, retaining negative measured responses; Pearson r = 0.810. This is a descriptive association between dependent matrix entries, not evidence that the response magnitudes are equal.</p><p>The D3 reference yields <strong>0.593 ± 0.035 nW</strong> at the ideal detector (mean ± one SE across three seeds). Intended-well shares span <strong>88.6–90.9%</strong> across LED rows; D3 floor CV is <strong>46.2%</strong>. The original power-matrix condition number is approximately 1.86. </p><figure class="model-v2-media" data-model-video="" data-static-video-number="2" id="optical-simulation-video"><div class="model-v2-media__label"><span>Optical paths and modulated detector traces</span><span>Original simulation · 20 s</span></div><iframe allow="autoplay; fullscreen; picture-in-picture" allowfullscreen="" aria-describedby="optical-simulation-video-caption" data-igem-video="" loading="lazy" src="https://video.igem.org/videos/embed/c9090c03-2106-4bf6-a336-b6be9dc2fe14?p2p=0" style="display:block;width:100%;height:auto;aspect-ratio:10 / 7;border:0" title="Optical paths and modulated detector traces"></iframe><figcaption id="optical-simulation-video-caption"><strong class="media-caption__label">Video 2. </strong><strong>100× slow motion:</strong> Panel A displays saved <strong>D3 sample trajectories only</strong>; the four LED indicators and ideal, conditional received and residual traces share one clock. Paths switch with LED state: they are not simulated photon flight times. Traces use green-equivalent nW. No calcium response, measured PCB timing or fabricated paths for the other wells are included. <a href="#model-data-availability">Data availability</a>.</figcaption><noscript><p><a href="#model-data-availability">Data and methods</a></p></noscript></figure><figure class="hw-v2-fig" data-static-figure-number="10" id="optical-floor-result"><a href="https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6460/wiki/hardware/calibration-refinement/simulation-floor-maps-red-63f25116a9f9.svg" rel="noopener" target="_blank"><img alt="Current four-well floor excitation maps" decoding="async" height="451" loading="lazy" src="https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6460/wiki/hardware/calibration-refinement/simulation-floor-maps-red-63f25116a9f9.svg" width="870"/></a><figcaption><strong class="media-caption__label">Figure 10. </strong><strong>Simulation · spatial illumination.</strong> D3 illumination uses 600,000 excitation packets; collection uses 224 source positions with 65,536 packets each and 0.5 × 0.4 mm pixels. The D3 floor irradiance has spatial CV 46.2%. Collection-map standard errors and Wilson 95% bounds are saved in the source but are not plotted as error bars; median relative collection-map SE is 17.3%. These are Monte Carlo sampling uncertainties, not biological replicates or measured uniformity. Fixed well registration and spatially distributed standards remain necessary. Method precedent: separate illumination and collection maps in Burton et al. <a aria-label="Reference 2" class="cite" data-model-reference="ref-burton-2020" href="#ref-burton-2020" id="cite-burton-model-maps">[2]</a> <a href="#model-data-availability">Data availability</a>. Positive irradiance and collection efficiency are shown in red; white denotes zero.</figcaption></figure><p class="supplementary-data__link"><a href="#optical-transport-details">Methods and supporting data</a></p><p><a class="hw-v2-text-link" href="hardware.html#optical-question">Hardware: optical registration and the selected reader layout →</a></p></section>
-<section class="section-block model-v2-physical" data-reveal="" id="detector-interference"><span class="section-eyebrow">Hardware simulations / 04</span><h2 class="reading-heading--chapter">Filter choice: predicted signal and leakage</h2><p data-study-context="detector-interference">Frequency tagging cannot reject excitation leakage at the fluorescence modulation frequency. We compare filter rejection against signal retention: read the spectra, integrated currents and signal-to-noise estimates under the same source assumptions.</p><dl class="audit-nutshell"><div><dt>Inputs</dt><dd>Unfiltered Monte Carlo powers, source spectra and detector response</dd></div><div><dt>Method</dt><dd>Spectral weighting, synthetic current and four-frequency complex fits</dd></div><div><dt>Output</dt><dd>Signal retention, conditional noise and blank-drift sensitivity</dd></div><div><dt>Decision</dt><dd>Select one LEE 101 layer; verify headroom and blank stability</dd></div></dl><p>The comparison tests no filter, one or two LEE101 layers, and a constructed 500 nm OD4 longpass scenario. The model integrates wavelength-dependent source power, filter transmission and detector responsivity to predict photodiode current in nA under the stated optical assumptions.</p><p>Burton et al. also separate fluorescence collection from excitation leakage in a compact photometry reader. Here, LEE transmission comes from the vendor curve; the paper’s filter and device measurements are not used as LEE 101 performance data. <a aria-label="Reference 2" class="cite" data-model-reference="ref-burton-2020" href="#ref-burton-2020" id="cite-burton-model-filter">[2]</a></p><figure class="hw-v2-fig hw-design-result" data-static-figure-number="11" id="filter-spectra"><a href="https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6460/wiki/aerosense-assets-20261017/08868df468f7-12-filter-spectral-comparison.webp" rel="noopener" target="_blank"><img alt="Filter transmission, source spectra, retained photocurrent and conditional SNR" decoding="async" height="1360" loading="lazy" src="https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6460/wiki/aerosense-assets-20261017/08868df468f7-12-filter-spectral-comparison.webp" width="2210"/></a><figcaption><strong class="media-caption__label">Figure 11. </strong><strong>Simulation · spectral comparison.</strong> LEE uses sampled vendor data; the OD4 pointwise curve is constructed. Panel C shows relative integrated photocurrent on a logarithmic axis (0.01–200%); panel D gives the minimum analytic SNR across four channels for a 1% fluorescence change after an ideal stable blank. No experimental or Monte Carlo confidence bands are shown. <a href="#model-data-availability">Data availability</a>.</figcaption></figure><div aria-label="Four filter scenarios, same modeled sources; current retained relative to no filter" class="table-scroll audit-table" role="region" tabindex="0"><table class="goal-table hw-v2-table" data-model-table-number="1" id="model-filter-comparison-table"><caption><strong class="media-caption__label">Table 1. </strong>Modeled detector currents and ideal-blank analytic SNR for four filter scenarios under the same sources. LED and proxy-green currents are percentages of the no-filter case; SNR assumes a 1% fluorescence change and the stated idealized noise model.</caption><thead><tr><th scope="col">Detector screen</th><th scope="col">LED current (%)</th><th scope="col">Proxy-green current (%)</th><th scope="col">Raw peak (nA)</th><th scope="col">Ideal-blank analytic SNR for 1% ΔF</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope="row">No filter</th><td>100.000</td><td>100.00</td><td>104.363</td><td>9.09</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">LEE 101 ×1</th><td>2.987</td><td>70.43</td><td>4.087</td><td>16.14</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">LEE 101 ×2</th><td>0.410</td><td>51.27</td><td>1.220</td><td>12.77</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">500 nm OD4 ×1*</th><td>0.194</td><td>82.74</td><td>1.344</td><td>20.54</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Under the stated noise assumptions, one LEE 101 layer retains 70.43% of modeled proxy-green current with analytic SNR 16.14 for a 1% change. The two-layer and constructed OD4 cases compare signal retention with stronger rejection.</p><div class="model-v2-result"><h3 class="reading-heading--topic">Selected design</h3><p>Use one LEE 101 layer in the reader. Retain raw I/Q, independent illuminated blanks and a signed phase reference; do not automatically apply the simulated mixing matrix.</p></div><figure class="hw-v2-fig hw-design-result" data-static-figure-number="12" id="filter-comparison-video"><span aria-hidden="true" class="anchor-alias" id="filter-waveforms"></span><a href="https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6460/wiki/aerosense-assets-20261017/caf054eceb34-13-filter-waveform-comparison.webp" rel="noopener" target="_blank"><img alt="Four filter scenarios with raw current, residual and corrected AC on different vertical scales" decoding="async" height="1800" loading="lazy" src="https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6460/wiki/aerosense-assets-20261017/caf054eceb34-13-filter-waveform-comparison.webp" width="2550"/></a><figcaption><strong class="media-caption__label">Figure 12. </strong><strong>Simulation · filter waveforms.</strong> The first 200 ms of four filter scenarios under identical source powers, clock and noise seeds. Columns show raw and ideal current, raw residual including DC and excitation leakage, and blank-corrected AC at four fundamentals. Vertical scales differ; OD4 is a constructed filter scenario. <a href="#model-data-availability">Data availability</a>.</figcaption></figure><figure class="hw-v2-fig hw-design-result" data-static-figure-number="13" id="filter-drift"><a href="https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6460/wiki/aerosense-assets-20261017/10b8ba0abf49-14-filter-residual-and-drift.webp" rel="noopener" target="_blank"><img alt="Blank-corrected residual and modeled sensitivity to excitation-leakage drift" decoding="async" height="935" loading="lazy" src="https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6460/wiki/aerosense-assets-20261017/10b8ba0abf49-14-filter-residual-and-drift.webp" width="2380"/></a><figcaption><strong class="media-caption__label">Figure 13. </strong><strong>Simulation · stability requirement.</strong> Left: one seeded corrected AC residual, in pA, on the same four-frequency basis. Right: deterministic blue I/Q gain drift relative to a hypothetical 1% fluorescence change; the dotted 0.1 line is an example engineering budget, not a validated acceptance limit. Fixed optical mean powers and a constructed OD4 scenario are used; no hardware drift is measured, and these curves are not uncertainty intervals. <a href="#model-data-availability">Data availability</a>.</figcaption></figure><p class="supplementary-data__link"><a href="#detector-interference-details">Methods and supporting data</a></p><p><a class="hw-v2-text-link" href="hardware.html#readout-background-result">Hardware: selected single-layer filter comparison →</a></p></section>
-<section class="section-block model-v2-physical" data-reveal="" id="model-to-build"><span class="section-eyebrow">Hardware simulations / 05</span><h2 class="reading-heading--chapter">What the models changed</h2><div aria-label="From computational evidence to a build decision" class="table-scroll" role="region" tabindex="0"><table class="hw-v2-table" data-model-table-number="2" id="table-3"><caption><strong class="media-caption__label">Table 2. </strong>Computational evidence and the resulting gas-flow, optical and membrane design decisions.</caption><thead><tr><th scope="col">Question</th><th scope="col">Evidence</th><th scope="col">Decision</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope="row">Can gas direction change without another part?</th><td>Shelf/open comparison, including worse exchange symmetry</td><td>Keep the integrated shelf and eight selected prints</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Which flow gives useful gas wash-in?</th><td>26 current surface cases and the 28-point operating-band sweep</td><td>Select 3 mL/min conditionally against declared criteria</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Does a finite PD collect modeled fluorescence?</th><td>MC geometry and acceptance maps</td><td>Retain the carrier as a test candidate; preserve filter / PD registration</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Are frequency tags independent wells?</th><td>Nonzero off-diagonal optical contributions</td><td>Preserve complex channels and measure a mixing matrix</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Does lock-in reject synchronous excitation?</th><td>Per-LED leakage and conditional channel bias</td><td>Select one LEE 101 layer and an independent illuminated blank; measure actual headroom</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p><a href="entrepreneurship.html#usability-manual-revision">How remaining evidence affects adoption</a></p><p><a class="hw-v2-text-link" href="hardware.html#mechanical-package">Hardware: resulting apparatus and assembly →</a></p></section>
-<section class="section-block" id="hardware-model-discussion"><span class="section-eyebrow">Hardware simulations / Discussion</span><h2 class="reading-heading--chapter">Discussion</h2><p>Optical geometry, filtering and transport impose different limits. Nonzero optical mixing calls for a measured channel-mixing matrix; LED-synchronous leakage calls for an illuminated blank. Gas-surface arrival establishes when the chamber supplies a well, while liquid diffusion determines the separate path to bottom-attached cells. A single end-to-end response time cannot be inferred by substituting one for another.</p><p>The shelf is a compact way to redirect flow, not the fastest geometry in every comparison. The selected 3 mL/min condition provides timing margin against the stated 60 s criterion; grid sensitivity makes a near-threshold 2.5 mL/min result less decisive. Together with the membrane sweep, these comparisons favor measurable delivery and a simple no-film baseline over adding uncharacterized layers.</p><p>The optical and filter results are predictions under their stated source and noise assumptions. The constructed OD4 disc also differs from the selected film in mounting geometry; its modeled performance does not establish compatibility with the reader.</p><p>The gas calculation ends above the liquid surface; this separate layer model describes subsequent transport. Its diffusivities and partition coefficients are sensitivity inputs. No-film delivery and recovery at the cell plane provide the physical comparison for any proposed membrane.</p><p>Physical checks must cover blank and dye calibration, actual detector/filter response, one fluorescent source well at a time, LED-synchronous background and noise, and assembled-path flow, pressure and interface concentration history. These measurements test the design decisions rather than being supplied by the simulations.</p><p>The four strong same-well entries contribute substantially to this association. Comparing only the 12 cross-well entries gives Pearson r = 0.257, so the overall correlation does not establish quantitative agreement of the leakage pattern. The liquid-standard and floor-layer source conditions also differ, and the measured response includes electronic effects.</p><p>The SE excludes uncertain LED power, resin optics, detector orientation, filter-angle response and cellular expression.</p><p>The predicted photodiode currents describe the optical input; they do not include measured electronics or cell responses.</p></section>
+<section class="section-block model-v2-physical" data-reveal="" id="optical-transport"><span class="section-eyebrow">Hardware simulations / 03</span><h2 class="reading-heading--chapter">Trace excitation and fluorescence</h2><p data-study-context="optical-transport">We varied optical geometry to test fluorescence collection and source-well mixing at the shared detector. Compare collection with spatial variation, then follow each LED row across the mixing matrix to separate intended-well signal from crosstalk.</p><dl class="audit-nutshell"><div><dt>Inputs</dt><dd>CAD, 500 µW LED-on reference, assumed optical properties</dd></div><div><dt>Method</dt><dd>Finite-detector Monte Carlo photon transport</dd></div><div><dt>Output</dt><dd>Collection, spatial variation and well mixing</dd></div><div><dt>Decision</dt><dd>Fix registration and calibrate each well</dd></div></dl><p>Geometry reference. The 470/520 nm study and path movie use monochromatic transport. The separate spectral comparison weights unfiltered Monte Carlo inputs by wavelength; the two studies report different power and noise quantities. <a href="#detector-interference">spectral filter comparison</a></p><p>The Monte Carlo geometry contains four registered wells, a finite offset photodiode, a finite emission filter and specified resin/liquid interfaces. Excitation and fluorescence packets are scored separately at nominal wavelengths of 470 and 520 nm. The reference source is a 20 µm layer at the well floor with absorption 0.5 mm<sup>−1</sup>, quantum yield 1 and 500 µW LED-on power. Ideal clear resin is an explicit assumption, not a measured material specification.</p><p>The optical maps follow Burton et al.’s separation of excitation fluence and detector acceptance (Fig. 2H–J). AeroSense computes its own four-well geometry; none of the paper’s device performance is transferred. <a aria-label="Reference 2" class="cite" data-model-reference="ref-burton-2020" href="#ref-burton-2020" id="cite-burton-model-method">[2]</a></p><figure class="hw-v2-fig" data-static-figure-number="8" id="model-optical-parameter-sweep"><a href="https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6460/wiki/hardware/expanded-verification/01-parameter-sweep-d-channels-10d0b10cbe4c.webp" rel="noopener" target="_blank"><img alt="Current optical parameter sweep with three-seed uncertainty" decoding="async" loading="lazy" src="https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6460/wiki/hardware/expanded-verification/01-parameter-sweep-d-channels-10d0b10cbe4c.webp"/></a><figcaption><strong class="media-caption__label">Figure 8. </strong>Current optical sensitivity study. Every comparison uses the stated geometry and source assumptions; error bars represent finite Monte Carlo sampling.</figcaption></figure><figure class="hw-v2-fig" data-static-figure-number="9" id="well-mixing-result"><a href="https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6460/wiki/review-resources/optical-calibration/canonical-simulation.svg" rel="noopener" target="_blank"><img alt="Canonical optical simulation matrix: row-normalized response percentages for readout D1 to D4 and source wells D1 to D4" decoding="async" loading="lazy" src="https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6460/wiki/review-resources/optical-calibration/canonical-simulation.svg"/></a><figcaption><strong class="media-caption__label">Figure 9. </strong><strong>Simulation · canonical optical mixing.</strong> This is the same floor-layer design model used in Hardware. Each row is normalized to 100%; for example, D4 own-well power 0.657 nW becomes 90.9% of its row total. Rows are LED / readout channels and columns are fluorescent source wells. The original three-seed matrix and its 20 µm source layer, absorption coefficient 0.5 mm⁻¹ and geometry are unchanged. <a href="hardware.html#reader-simulation-comparison">Measured comparison</a></figcaption></figure><p>The measured comparison pairs all 16 corresponding entries of the simulated and measured row-normalized matrices. The scatter plot uses simulation on the horizontal axis and measurement on the vertical axis, retaining negative measured responses; Pearson r = 0.810. This is a descriptive association between dependent matrix entries, not evidence that the response magnitudes are equal.</p><p>The D3 reference yields <strong>0.593 ± 0.035 nW</strong> at the ideal detector (mean ± one SE across three seeds). Intended-well shares span <strong>88.6–90.9%</strong> across LED rows; D3 floor CV is <strong>46.2%</strong>. The original power-matrix condition number is approximately 1.86. </p><figure class="model-v2-media" data-model-video="" data-static-video-number="2" id="optical-simulation-video"><div class="model-v2-media__label"><span>Optical paths and modulated detector traces</span><span>Original simulation · 20 s</span></div><iframe allow="autoplay; fullscreen; picture-in-picture" allowfullscreen="" aria-describedby="optical-simulation-video-caption" data-igem-video="" loading="lazy" src="https://video.igem.org/videos/embed/c9090c03-2106-4bf6-a336-b6be9dc2fe14?p2p=0" style="display:block;width:100%;height:auto;aspect-ratio:10 / 7;border:0" title="Optical paths and modulated detector traces"></iframe><figcaption id="optical-simulation-video-caption"><strong class="media-caption__label">Video 2. </strong><strong>100× slow motion:</strong> Panel A displays saved <strong>D3 sample trajectories only</strong>; the four LED indicators and ideal, conditional received and residual traces share one clock. Paths switch with LED state: they are not simulated photon flight times. Traces use green-equivalent nW. No calcium response, measured PCB timing or fabricated paths for the other wells are included. <a href="#model-data-availability">Data availability</a>.</figcaption><noscript><p><a href="#model-data-availability">Data and methods</a></p></noscript></figure><figure class="hw-v2-fig" data-static-figure-number="10" id="optical-floor-result"><a href="https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6460/wiki/hardware/calibration-refinement/simulation-floor-maps-red-63f2511