c current and four-frequency complex fits</dd></div><div><dt>Output</dt><dd>Signal retention, conditional noise and blank-drift sensitivity</dd></div><div><dt>Decision</dt><dd>Select one LEE 101 layer; verify headroom and blank stability</dd></div></dl><p>The comparison tests no filter, one or two LEE101 layers, and a constructed 500 nm OD4 longpass scenario. The model integrates wavelength-dependent source power, filter transmission and detector responsivity to predict photodiode current in nA under the stated optical assumptions.</p><p>Burton et al. also separate fluorescence collection from excitation leakage in a compact photometry reader. Here, LEE transmission comes from the vendor curve; the paper’s filter and device measurements are not used as LEE 101 performance data. <a aria-label="Reference 2" class="cite" data-model-reference="ref-burton-2020" href="#ref-burton-2020" id="cite-burton-model-filter">[2]</a></p><figure class="hw-v2-fig hw-design-result" data-static-figure-number="11" id="filter-spectra"><a href="https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6460/wiki/aerosense-assets-20261017/08868df468f7-12-filter-spectral-comparison.webp" rel="noopener" target="_blank"><img alt="Filter transmission, source spectra, retained photocurrent and conditional SNR" decoding="async" height="1360" loading="lazy" src="https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6460/wiki/aerosense-assets-20261017/08868df468f7-12-filter-spectral-comparison.webp" width="2210"/></a><figcaption><strong class="media-caption__label">Figure 11. </strong><strong>Simulation · spectral comparison.</strong> LEE uses sampled vendor data; the OD4 pointwise curve is constructed. Panel C shows relative integrated photocurrent on a logarithmic axis (0.01–200%); panel D gives the minimum analytic SNR across four channels for a 1% fluorescence change after an ideal stable blank. No experimental or Monte Carlo confidence bands are shown. <a href="#model-data-availability">Data availability</a>.</figcaption></figure><div aria-label="Four filter scenarios, same modeled sources; current retained relative to no filter" class="table-scroll audit-table" role="region" tabindex="0"><table class="goal-table hw-v2-table" data-model-table-number="1" id="model-filter-comparison-table"><caption><strong class="media-caption__label">Table 1. </strong>Modeled detector currents and ideal-blank analytic SNR for four filter scenarios under the same sources. LED and proxy-green currents are percentages of the no-filter case; SNR assumes a 1% fluorescence change and the stated idealized noise model.</caption><thead><tr><th scope="col">Detector screen</th><th scope="col">LED current (%)</th><th scope="col">Proxy-green current (%)</th><th scope="col">Raw peak (nA)</th><th scope="col">Ideal-blank analytic SNR for 1% ΔF</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope="row">No filter</th><td>100.000</td><td>100.00</td><td>104.363</td><td>9.09</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">LEE 101 ×1</th><td>2.987</td><td>70.43</td><td>4.087</td><td>16.14</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">LEE 101 ×2</th><td>0.410</td><td>51.27</td><td>1.220</td><td>12.77</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">500 nm OD4 ×1*</th><td>0.194</td><td>82.74</td><td>1.344</td><td>20.54</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Under the stated noise assumptions, one LEE 101 layer retains 70.43% of modeled proxy-green current with analytic SNR 16.14 for a 1% change. The two-layer and constructed OD4 cases compare signal retention with stronger rejection.</p><div class="model-v2-result"><h3 class="reading-heading--topic">Selected design</h3><p>Use one LEE 101 layer in the reader. Retain raw I/Q, independent illuminated blanks and a signed phase reference; do not automatically apply the simulated mixing matrix.</p></div><figure class="hw-v2-fig hw-design-result" data-static-figure-number="12" id="filter-comparison-video"><span aria-hidden="true" class="anchor-alias" id="filter-waveforms"></span><a href="https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6460/wiki/aerosense-assets-20261017/caf054eceb34-13-filter-waveform-comparison.webp" rel="noopener" target="_blank"><img alt="Four filter scenarios with raw current, residual and corrected AC on different vertical scales" decoding="async" height="1800" loading="lazy" src="https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6460/wiki/aerosense-assets-20261017/caf054eceb34-13-filter-waveform-comparison.webp" width="2550"/></a><figcaption><strong class="media-caption__label">Figure 12. </strong><strong>Simulation · filter waveforms.</strong> The first 200 ms of four filter scenarios under identical source powers, clock and noise seeds. Columns show raw and ideal current, raw residual including DC and excitation leakage, and blank-corrected AC at four fundamentals. Vertical scales differ; OD4 is a constructed filter scenario. <a href="#model-data-availability">Data availability</a>.</figcaption></figure><figure class="hw-v2-fig hw-design-result" data-static-figure-number="13" id="filter-drift"><a href="https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6460/wiki/aerosense-assets-20261017/10b8ba0abf49-14-filter-residual-and-drift.webp" rel="noopener" target="_blank"><img alt="Blank-corrected residual and modeled sensitivity to excitation-leakage drift" decoding="async" height="935" loading="lazy" src="https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6460/wiki/aerosense-assets-20261017/10b8ba0abf49-14-filter-residual-and-drift.webp" width="2380"/></a><figcaption><strong class="media-caption__label">Figure 13. </strong><strong>Simulation · stability requirement.</strong> Left: one seeded corrected AC residual, in pA, on the same four-frequency basis. Right: deterministic blue I/Q gain drift relative to a hypothetical 1% fluorescence change; the dotted 0.1 line is an example engineering budget, not a validated acceptance limit. Fixed optical mean powers and a constructed OD4 scenario are used; no hardware drift is measured, and these curves are not uncertainty intervals. <a href="#model-data-availability">Data availability</a>.</figcaption></figure></section></section>
+<section class="section-block model-v2-physical" data-reveal="" id="model-to-build"><span class="section-eyebrow">Hardware simulations / 05</span><h2 class="reading-heading--chapter">What the models changed</h2><div aria-label="From computational evidence to a build decision" class="table-scroll" role="region" tabindex="0"><table class="hw-v2-table" data-model-table-number="2" id="table-3"><caption><strong class="media-caption__label">Table 2. </strong>Computational evidence and the resulting gas-flow, optical and membrane design decisions.</caption><thead><tr><th scope="col">Question</th><th scope="col">Evidence</th><th scope="col">Decision</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope="row">Can gas direction change without another part?</th><td>Shelf/open comparison, including worse exchange symmetry</td><td>Keep the integrated shelf and eight selected prints</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Which flow gives useful gas wash-in?</th><td>26 current surface cases and the 28-point operating-band sweep</td><td>Select 3 mL/min conditionally against declared criteria</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Does a finite PD collect modeled fluorescence?</th><td>MC geometry and acceptance maps</td><td>Retain the carrier as a test candidate; preserve filter / PD registration</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Are frequency tags independent wells?</th><td>Nonzero off-diagonal optical contributions</td><td>Preserve complex channels and measure a mixing matrix</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Does lock-in reject synchronous excitation?</th><td>Per-LED leakage and conditional channel bias</td><td>Select one LEE 101 layer and an independent illuminated blank; measure actual headroom</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p><a class="hw-v2-text-link" href="hardware.html#mechanical-package">Hardware: resulting apparatus and assembly →</a></p></section>
+<section class="section-block" id="hardware-model-discussion"><span class="section-eyebrow">Hardware simulations / Discussion</span><h2 class="reading-heading--chapter">Discussion</h2><p>Gas-surface arrival and transport to bottom-attached cells are separate processes. The shelf redirects flow without being fastest in every comparison. The selected 3 mL/min condition provides margin against the 60 s criterion; grid sensitivity weakens the near-threshold 2.5 mL/min result. The membrane sweep supports a no-film baseline until delivery and recovery at the cell plane are measured.</p><p>Optical mixing requires a measured channel matrix, while LED-synchronous leakage requires an illuminated blank. Predicted currents and Monte Carlo SE depend on the assumed source, material, detector and filter properties; they do not include measured electronics or cellular variation. The constructed OD4 scenario also uses a different mounting geometry from the selected film.</p><p>Strong same-well entries drive much of the simulated–measured matrix association: the 12 cross-well entries alone give Pearson r = 0.257. Different source distributions and electronic effects prevent treating this as quantitative leakage agreement. Blank and dye calibration, single-source-well tests, detector headroom and noise, and assembled-path flow and concentration measurements are the next checks.</p></section>
 <section class="section-block" id="hardware-model-conclusion"><span class="section-eyebrow">Hardware simulations / Conclusion</span><h2 class="reading-heading--chapter">Conclusion</h2><p>The calculations support one LEE 101 layer, an integral shelf and a conditional 3 mL/min operating point. They also identify liquid-path length as a potential bottleneck. These design choices now have explicit optical, gas-delivery and cell-plane measurements against which they can be tested.</p></section>
